To link your Next.js app to an SQL database hosted on AWS, you will need to set up a database on AWS, connect to it from your Next.js app, and use an ORM (Object-Relational Mapping) tool like Prisma for ease of database interaction.

Step 1: Set Up an AWS RDS Instance
Create an RDS Instance:

Log in to your AWS Management Console.
Navigate to the RDS service.
Click on "Create database".
Choose a database creation method (Standard Create).
Select the database engine (e.g., MySQL, PostgreSQL).
Configure your instance (DB instance class, storage, etc.).
Set up master username and password.
Configure additional settings (VPC, subnet, security groups, etc.).
Launch the instance.
Configure Security Groups:

Ensure the security group associated with your RDS instance allows inbound traffic on the port your database uses (e.g., 3306 for MySQL, 5432 for PostgreSQL).
Add your local IP address or EC2 instance IP address to the security group to allow access.
Step 2: Set Up Prisma in Your Next.js Project
Install Prisma:

bash
Copy code
npm install @prisma/client
npm install prisma --save-dev
Initialize Prisma:

bash
Copy code
npx prisma init
Configure Prisma:

In the prisma/.env file, add your database connection string:

env
Copy code
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://username:password@hostname:port/database_name"
Replace username, password, hostname, port, and database_name with your RDS instance details.

Define Your Data Model:

Edit prisma/schema.prisma to define your data model:

prisma
Copy code
datasource db {
  provider = "postgresql"
  url      = env("DATABASE_URL")
}

generator client {
  provider = "prisma-client-js"
}

model Pub {
  id               Int      @id @default(autoincrement())
  name             String
  description      String
  image            String
  dateRating       Float
  showsSports      Boolean
  cozyLivelyRating Int
}
Migrate Your Database:

bash
Copy code
npx prisma migrate dev --name init
Step 3: Use Prisma in Your Next.js App
Create a Prisma Client Instance:

Create a lib/prisma.js file:

javascript
Copy code
import { PrismaClient } from '@prisma/client';

const prisma = new PrismaClient();

export default prisma;
Fetch Data in Your API Route:

Update your API route to fetch data from the database using Prisma:

javascript
Copy code
// pages/api/pubs.js
import prisma from '../../lib/prisma';

export default async function handler(req, res) {
  const pubs = await prisma.pub.findMany();
  res.status(200).json(pubs);
}
Use the API Route in Your Pages:

Update your Home and Search pages to fetch data from the API route:

javascript
Copy code
// pages/index.js
import axios from 'axios';
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';
import PubList from '../components/PubList';
import NavBar from '../components/NavBar';

const Home = () => {
  const [pubs, setPubs] = useState([]);

  useEffect(() => {
    const fetchPubs = async () => {
      const result = await axios('/api/pubs');
      setPubs(result.data);
    };

    fetchPubs();
  }, []);

  return (
    <div>
      <NavBar />
      <div className="container mx-auto">
        <h1 className="text-4xl font-bold text-center my-8">Pub Comparison</h1>
        <PubList pubs={pubs} />
      </div>
    </div>
  );
};

export default Home;
javascript
Copy code
// pages/search.js
import axios from 'axios';
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';
import PubList from '../components/PubList';
import NavBar from '../components/NavBar';

const Search = () => {
  const [pubs, setPubs] = useState([]);
  const [filteredPubs, setFilteredPubs] = useState([]);
  const [dateRating, setDateRating] = useState('');
  const [showsSports, setShowsSports] = useState(false);
  const [cozyLivelyRating, setCozyLivelyRating] = useState(3);

  useEffect(() => {
    const fetchPubs = async () => {
      const result = await axios('/api/pubs');
      setPubs(result.data);
      setFilteredPubs(result.data); // Initialize with all pubs
    };

    fetchPubs();
  }, []);

  const handleSearch = () => {
    const filtered = pubs.filter(pub => 
      pub.dateRating >= dateRating &&
      pub.showsSports === showsSports &&
      pub.cozyLivelyRating === cozyLivelyRating
    );
    setFilteredPubs(filtered);
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <NavBar />
      <div className="container mx-auto">
        <h1 className="text-4xl font-bold text-center my-8">Search Pubs</h1>
        <div className="flex justify-center my-4">
          <input
            type="number"
            placeholder="Minimum date rating"
            value={dateRating}
            onChange={(e) => setDateRating(e.target.value)}
            className="border rounded py-2 px-4"
          />
          <label className="ml-4">
            <input
              type="checkbox"
              checked={showsSports}
              onChange={(e) => setShowsSports(e.target.checked)}
              className="mr-2"
            />
            Shows Sports
          </label>
          <label className="ml-4 flex items-center">
            Cozy-Lively Rating
            <input
              type="range"
              min="1"
              max="5"
              value={cozyLivelyRating}
              onChange={(e) => setCozyLivelyRating(parseInt(e.target.value))}
              className="ml-2"
            />
            <span className="ml-2">{cozyLivelyRating}</span>
          </label>
          <button
            onClick={handleSearch}
            className="bg-teal-500 text-white py-2 px-4 rounded ml-2"
          >
            Search
          </button>
        </div>
        <PubList pubs={filteredPubs} />
      </div>
    </div>
  );
};

export default Search;
